<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/advanced/spectroscopy-and-structure">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Modern chemists rarely see molecules — they <strong>interrogate them with light and
    magnets</strong>. Molecules absorb very particular frequencies: bond vibrations in the
    infrared, nuclear flips in radio waves (NMR), electron jumps in UV-visible. Each technique
    answers a different structural question, and together they read molecular structure like
    text — no crystal ball required.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="an infrared fingerprint — the dips mark bond vibrations: O–H, C=O, C–H each singe at their own frequency" widget="plotter" config={specCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the three deep dips: ~3300 cm⁻¹ is an O–H stretch (alcohol or water), ~2900 cm⁻¹ the
    C–H backbone, ~1750 cm⁻¹ the C=O of a carbonyl. A functional-group table converts the dips
    into structure — this spectrum is basically vinegar introducing itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The four instruments</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mass spectrometry:</strong> smash the molecule, weigh the fragments. The heaviest peak is the molecular mass; the fragment pattern is a fingerprint. It told us the Moon's soil composition from a few grams of Apollo sample.</li>
    <li><strong>NMR:</strong> hydrogen nuclei resonate in a magnetic field at frequencies shifted by their neighbourhood — a map of the hydrogen (and carbon) skeleton. MRI in hospitals is the same physics aimed at water in tissue.</li>
    <li><strong>IR:</strong> bond vibrations (above). Cheap, fast, the first question asked of any new compound.</li>
    <li><strong>X-ray crystallography:</strong> diffract X-rays off a crystal and reconstruct the electron density — the technique that revealed DNA's double helix (Photo 51) and most of the Protein Data Bank.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Pavia, Introduction to Spectroscopy; Franklin & Gosling, Nature (1953); Protein Data Bank.</p>
</TopicPage>
